  "method": "One data owner in the four-worker configuration serving eight established downloads. The 128-event control and the ordinary 24-event arm use the same relay revision, 24-event polls and 256 KiB cap. On the selected successful bulk write the fixture queues a handoff on that owner's actual serial dispatch queue. At entry it stages a fresh connected socketpair flow and writes its one-byte reply. Measure bytes relayed after enqueue up to entry and up to forwarding the reply. The fixture explicitly submits follow-on drain callbacks.",
  "guardrails": "Every trial validates 8 MiB of per-flow ordered bulk, one fresh byte, forward FIN, reverse marker/FIN and retirement of all nine streams. No clocks, TCP, guest, per-process CPU or scheduled-arrival percentiles. Results are deterministic fixed-work counts for the five chosen injection positions, not a sampled arrival distribution.",
  "verdict": "The shorter drain permits earlier entry at three of five positions. It removes 2 MiB before handoff entry in each case, and saves zero bulk bytes before the actual fresh reply at every position. Both repeated arm orders agree.",
  "next_check": "Retain 128 events in the worker-count comparison. In a separately admitted callback trial, distinguish enqueue-to-adopt from adopt-to-first-byte before crediting earlier handoff entry with any reduction in scheduled-arrival latency.",
